{
  "description": "Flow eval: does gmstack carry a task through its playbook's steps, not just route it? Each case starts from a copy of the fixture ('fixture': 'project') or an empty git repo ('empty'), may run a 'setup' shell command (committed with the copy as the baseline, e.g. to plant a bug) and a 'wip' command (run after the baseline and left uncommitted), then runs a real, writable task on a throwaway copy of the fixture and checks the transcript and the resulting files for one marker per playbook step. A step passes when any of its markers is found, at least 'min' times when the step sets it (default 1). Markers: 'skill:<name>' (the gmstack skill was invoked, or its SKILL.md read), 'read:<regex>' (a file whose path matches was read), 'bash:<regex>' (a shell command matched), 'agent' (a subagent was spawned), 'tool:<name>' (that tool was called), 'changed:<regex>' (a tracked or untracked file whose path matches was added or modified, committed or not), 'wrote:<regex>' (the Write or Edit tool targeted a matching absolute path; sees git-ignored files). A leading '!' inverts a marker: the step passes when it was never seen. Completion rate = steps passed / steps checked.",
  "cases": [
